+**As landed: a root-edge delta (01a17a40-4915, perigrin's ruling R-e).**
+Repair does not shift reused nodes at all. Offsets stay absolute inside
+each top-level statement, as the parser built them, and only the root's
+child edges carry a delta: `shifts[i]` on the SourceFile root, nil after a
+full parse. Repair builds a new root whose children are the old root's,
+the re-parsed statement in place of the anchor, and moves the deltas of
+the edges after it by the edit's growth. No node under any edge is copied
+or written, so the old and new trees share every statement but one and
+both stay valid: a statement can sit at two offsets in two trees.
+
+The cost is that a node no longer knows its own file offset. `Start()`,
+`End()`, `InnerStart()` and `InnerEnd()` read the offsets a node holds,
+which are the file's only when its statement's edge carries no delta; a
+walk from the root adds `ChildShift(i)` at the top level and nothing
+below it. Inside `internal/parse` the readers that take a tree Repair may
+have built do so (TreeDiff, SourceText, Repair's own triage, the
+unclosed-opener pass), and the whole-file readers that slice the source
+by span (Canon, Sites) read `absolute(root)`, a copy of only the
+statements whose edge has a delta. Outside it, readers use the absolute
+spans `lower.AssignIDs` records during its walk, never a node's own
+offset, and no cursor type enters a signature.
+
+The underflow trap above cannot arise: nothing is shifted, and a delta is
+added only when an offset is read. Measured over 200 edits of
+`latencyEdits` on edit5k.pl, the splice stage went from 9.19 ms p50 and
+78.4 ms p99, copying every reused node, to the numbers recorded in plan §5
+M2's `#### Measured splice cost`. Arena allocation is out of scope under
+this representation, as it was under both options the ruling chose between.
+
